{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git status --short; tail -75 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790808100000-architect-linker-over-dht-design-doc.claude-fable-5-1.stdout-r5.log; sed -n '475,490p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v475; sed -n '508,595p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v508; sed -n '1218,1265p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v1218\"","aggregated_output":" M docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md\n M docs/design/yin.repl.link-policy.md\n M docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md\n?? docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md\nThe remaining r4 finding is closed in `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md`, now revision r5. Only docs changed, and nothing is staged or committed.\n\nI took the first option and kept in-flight sharing for overflow publications. Excluding them would have meant a second request for an address already on its way.\n\n- **Event rule (section 5.5.3).** `:republished` now reports any result change after the first report, whatever wrote the ledger. In every step that writes to a reported publication's ledger, the node recomputes the result and whether repair has ended, and emits the event if either changed. The doc states that no result ever changes without an event.\n- **Overflow case named.** A publication reported `publications-full` that shares an already outstanding request is republished in the step that request's sent fact arrives, before any repair cycle of its own.\n- **No-event rule tightened.** A step that changes ledger entries but neither the result nor the end of repair still produces no event.\n- **L1 acceptance.** The new test runs overflow, then a shared outstanding address, then the sent fact. It asserts:\n  - an unacknowledged first report with the shared address failed as `publications-full`;\n  - `:republished :acknowledged` in the fact's step;\n  - no second request for the address;\n  - no repair cycle ever opening for that publication.\n\n  A variant with a second unshared row asserts partial on the fact and acknowledged after its own repair. A shared failure fact asserts no event.\n   475\t  It is retired when it is `:acknowledged`, when its repair ends (5.5.4),\n   476\t  or when the node closes.\n   477\t- **After the first report, every blob is `sent` or `failed`.** A failed\n   478\t  blob whose repair try is queued or outstanding stays `failed`, with its\n   479\t  last reason, until a `/sent` arrives. So in every event of a publication\n   480\t  `:sent + (count :failed) = :blobs`.\n   481\t\n   482\t**Shared addresses.** One address has at most one queue entry and at most\n   483\tone outstanding request on the node, whatever number of live publications\n   484\thold it.\n   485\t\n   486\t- When an address's request settles, the outcome is written to the ledger\n   487\t  of **every live publication** that holds the address as `waiting` or\n   488\t  `failed`: `/sent` makes it `sent` in each; a failure makes it `failed`\n   489\t  with that reason in each.\n   490\t- A publication announced while an address it holds is already queued or\n   508\t  repair its place, and no amount of repair denies a fresh write its place.\n   509\t- **A full fresh queue.** A fresh address that arrives when the fresh queue\n   510\t  is full is **not admitted**: it is `failed` at once with reason\n   511\t  `:dao.space.dht/backlog-full`. Its local copy stays. Nothing is evicted to\n   512\t  make room.\n   513\t- **The repair queue is never full for a publication's batch.** A repairing\n   514\t  publication holds at most `:repair-batch` (default 64) addresses in the\n   515\t  repair queue at once, and at most `:max-repairing` publications repair,\n   516\t  so the capacity above always has room for every repairing publication's\n   517\t  batch (5.5.4).\n   518\t- **Open publications are bounded.** At most `:max-open` (default 16)\n   519\t  publications may await their first report. An announcement that would\n   520\t  make one more does not wait and displaces nothing: every address of the\n   521\t  new publication is `failed` at once with reason\n   522\t  `:dao.space.dht/publications-full`, none is `waiting`, its first report\n   523\t  is made in that step, and it continues under repair. An address it shares\n   524\t  with an attempt already in progress is `failed` like the rest, and takes\n   525\t  that attempt's outcome when it settles, by the shared-address rule of\n   526\t  5.5.1.\n   527\t- **What a node retains** is therefore bounded by composition data alone:\n   528\t  at most `:max-open + :max-repairing` live publications, each ledger at\n   529\t  most the ledger ring's capacity in addresses; at most `:max-backlog`\n   530\t  fresh and `:max-repairing × :repair-batch` repair queue entries; at most\n   531\t  `max-pending-writes` requests outstanding. `:max-repairing` bounds\n   532\t  repairing publications only.\n   533\t- **Release.** Each step appends replicate requests while the number\n   534\t  outstanding is below the DHT's `max-pending-writes`. The node never has\n   535\t  more outstanding than that bound, so its own writes never draw `/busy`.\n   536\t- **Fairness between the queues.** While the repair queue is non-empty,\n   537\t  `:repair-slots` (default 16) of the outstanding bound are reserved for\n   538\t  it: fresh requests hold at most `max-pending-writes − repair-slots`\n   539\t  outstanding, repair requests at most `:repair-slots`. While the repair\n   540\t  queue is empty, fresh requests may use the whole bound. Neither queue can\n   541\t  starve the other, and repairs never take more than their share from a new\n   542\t  publication.\n   543\t- **Order.** Each queue is strictly FIFO. Fresh order is put order, so an\n   544\t  older publication's first pass precedes a newer one's. No publication is\n   545\t  prioritized.\n   546\t- **Outcomes.** One request has one outcome. `/sent` is `sent`. Every\n   547\t  `/unacknowledged` reason is `failed` with that reason. There is no\n   548\t  immediate second try: a retry is a repair (5.5.4).\n   549\t- **A solo or non-publishing node holds no backlog.** Every put is `failed`\n   550\t  at admission with `/solo` or `/unpublished`.\n   551\t- **A lost ledger-ring entry** (more puts in one window than the ring\n   552\t  holds) or a facts gap reports `:publication-unknown`, as today, and that\n   553\t  publication is not live.\n   554\t\n   555\t#### 5.5.3 The result and the events\n   556\t\n   557\t```clojure\n   558\t{:dao.space.dht/event :published | :republished\n   559\t :manifest m\n   560\t :result :acknowledged | :partial | :unacknowledged\n   561\t :blobs n            ; the ledger's size; constant for the publication\n   562\t :sent s             ; ledger entries sent\n   563\t :peers p            ; fewest peers any sent blob reached; absent when s = 0\n   564\t :failed [{:address a :reason r :peers k} ...]   ; every entry not sent\n   565\t :repairing? b       ; true while the node will retry this publication\n   566\t :ended reason}      ; only when repair ended without acknowledgement\n   567\t```\n   568\t\n   569\t| `:result` | Condition, over the whole ledger | What it promises |\n   570\t|---|---|---|\n   571\t| `:acknowledged` | Every blob sent. | Every address of the publication was handed to at least `ack-peers` peers. |\n   572\t| `:partial` | The manifest blob sent; at least one other blob not. | Peers can find the publication. The addresses under `:failed` are held and served by the publisher only. |\n   573\t| `:unacknowledged` | The manifest blob not sent. | Nothing beyond the local copy. `:failed` still lists every blob not sent. |\n   574\t\n   575\t- The result is always computed over the **whole ledger**. A blob sent in\n   576\t  the first pass and a blob sent by a repair count alike. A publication\n   577\t  whose manifest was sent in the first pass and whose rows were sent by\n   578\t  repair is `:acknowledged`.\n   579\t- `:failed` is complete, in address order.\n   580\t- `:acknowledged?` is removed, with no compatibility path.\n   581\t\n   582\t**Which event, when.** A publication produces:\n   583\t\n   584\t| Event | When | Times |\n   585\t|---|---|---|\n   586\t| `:published` | The **first report**: every blob has left `waiting`. | Exactly once. |\n   587\t| `:republished` | After the first report, the `:result` changed (to `:partial` or to `:acknowledged`), **whatever wrote the ledger**, or repair ended. A change that ends repair is one event. | Zero or more; at most two result changes and one end. |\n   588\t\n   589\t- **The cause of a change does not matter.** A ledger entry of a reported\n   590\t  publication is written by its own repair request, or by the fact of a\n   591\t  request it shares with another publication (5.5.1): a fresh request\n   592\t  another publication issued, or another publication's repair. In every\n   593\t  step that wrote to a reported publication's ledger, the node recomputes\n   594\t  its result and whether its repair has ended, and emits `:republished` if\n   595\t  either changed. No result ever changes without an event.\n  1218\t  original (manifest sent, one row refused): after the mesh heals and\n  1219\t  ticks pass `:repair-ticks`, exactly one `:republished` arrives with\n  1220\t  `:acknowledged`, `:sent` equal to `:blobs`, and the manifest's replicate\n  1221\t  request was issued once in total. An `:unacknowledged` original (manifest\n  1222\t  and rows refused): healing first the manifest's store, then the rest,\n  1223\t  yields `:republished :partial`, then `:republished :acknowledged`, and\n  1224\t  nothing after. A blob already `sent` is never requested again.\n  1225\t- **A peer that never accepts.** With every peer refusing or never\n  1226\t  answering stores, over sustained rounds and a long run of appended ticks:\n  1227\t  every first report is `:unacknowledged` with `:repairing? true`; **no\n  1228\t  event ever reports `:acknowledged`**; no `:republished` is produced\n  1229\t  except one `:ended :dao.space.dht/displaced` per publication beyond\n  1230\t  `:max-repairing`; the fresh queue never exceeds `:max-backlog` and the\n  1231\t  repair queue never exceeds `:max-repairing × :repair-batch`;\n  1232\t  outstanding requests never exceed `max-pending-writes`; repairing\n  1233\t  publications never exceed `:max-repairing`; the repair delay doubles to\n  1234\t  `:repair-max-ticks` and stays; no put ever waits; and `busy?` is false\n  1235\t  between cycles.\n  1236\t- **Retained ledgers stay bounded, measured separately.** Under the same\n  1237\t  dead network, with rounds announced faster than first reports arrive:\n  1238\t  publications awaiting a first report never exceed `:max-open`; the\n  1239\t  announcement beyond it is reported in the step that reads it, every blob\n  1240\t  `:dao.space.dht/publications-full`, `:repairing? true`, displacing\n  1241\t  nothing open; live publications never exceed `:max-open +\n  1242\t  :max-repairing`; and the total of ledger entries the node retains, summed\n  1243\t  over live publications, stays at or below that count times the ledger\n  1244\t  ring's capacity through the whole run. A case with many open publications\n  1245\t  sharing one queued address asserts the same total while the queue holds\n  1246\t  one entry.\n  1247\t- **Fairness of requests.** With the repair queue full of failing addresses\n  1248\t  and a new round arriving, the new round's first pass holds at least\n  1249\t  `max-pending-writes − repair-slots` outstanding and is reported while\n  1250\t  repairs continue; repair requests never exceed `:repair-slots`\n  1251\t  outstanding; with no repairs queued, a fresh round uses the whole bound.\n  1252\t- **Repair under sustained fresh writes.** One early publication has one\n  1253\t  row refused and is `:partial`. The peers then heal for that row. From\n  1254\t  before its first cycle is due, and without pause, fresh rounds keep the\n  1255\t  fresh queue at `:max-backlog`, their excess `:backlog-full`. The early\n  1256\t  publication's failed row is admitted to the repair queue in the step its\n  1257\t  cycle opens, receives its replicate request within the reserved slots\n  1258\t  while the fresh queue is still full, and the publication is reported\n  1259\t  `:republished :acknowledged`, all while fresh writes continue. The test\n  1260\t  fails if the repair is admitted only after the fresh queue drains.\n  1261\t- **Batches.** A publication with more retryable failures than\n  1262\t  `:repair-batch` never holds more than `:repair-batch` addresses in the\n  1263\t  repair queue, has every one of them requested within one cycle, and\n  1264\t  `:max-repairing` such publications repairing at once each make progress\n  1265\t  in every cycle.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
