{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '589,618p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v589; sed -n '1260,1305p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v1260\"","aggregated_output":"   589\t- **The cause of a change does not matter.** A ledger entry of a reported\n   590\t  publication is written by its own repair request, or by the fact of a\n   591\t  request it shares with another publication (5.5.1): a fresh request\n   592\t  another publication issued, or another publication's repair. In every\n   593\t  step that wrote to a reported publication's ledger, the node recomputes\n   594\t  its result and whether its repair has ended, and emits `:republished` if\n   595\t  either changed. No result ever changes without an event.\n   596\t- This covers a publication reported `:dao.space.dht/publications-full`\n   597\t  (5.5.2) that shares an address whose request was already outstanding:\n   598\t  when that request's `/sent` arrives, the entry becomes `sent`, and if the\n   599\t  result is now `:partial` or `:acknowledged` the publication is\n   600\t  `:republished` in that step, before any repair cycle of its own.\n   601\t- `:repairing?` is true in an event exactly when the result is not\n   602\t  `:acknowledged` and repair has not ended.\n   603\t- A step that changes ledger entries but neither the result nor whether\n   604\t  repair has ended produces **no event**. A publication that never\n   605\t  improves is silent after its first report.\n   606\t- **Final acknowledgement** is the one `:republished` (or the first\n   607\t  `:published`) whose `:result` is `:acknowledged`. Nothing follows it.\n   608\t- `:ended` is one of `:dao.space.dht/terminal` (every remaining failure is\n   609\t  terminal), `:dao.space.dht/cancelled`, `:dao.space.dht/displaced` (5.5.4).\n   610\t\n   611\t#### 5.5.4 Automatic repair (owner decision 5: \"Automatic retry while open\")\n   612\t\n   613\tA publication whose first report is not `:acknowledged` is repaired by the\n   614\tnode, in the background, for as long as the node is open.\n   615\t\n   616\t- **What is retried.** A `failed` blob whose reason is **retryable**:\n   617\t  `/too-few-peers`, `/busy`, `:dao.space.dht/backlog-full`,\n   618\t  `:dao.space.dht/publications-full`. A blob that is `sent` is never sent\n  1260\t  fails if the repair is admitted only after the fresh queue drains.\n  1261\t- **Batches.** A publication with more retryable failures than\n  1262\t  `:repair-batch` never holds more than `:repair-batch` addresses in the\n  1263\t  repair queue, has every one of them requested within one cycle, and\n  1264\t  `:max-repairing` such publications repairing at once each make progress\n  1265\t  in every cycle.\n  1266\t- **Overflow, a shared outstanding address, then `/sent`.** With\n  1267\t  `:max-open` publications open and the request for address `a`\n  1268\t  outstanding for one of them, a further publication whose only blobs are\n  1269\t  its manifest and `a`, the manifest being `a`, is announced. Its first\n  1270\t  report, in that step, is `:unacknowledged` with `a` under `:failed` as\n  1271\t  `:dao.space.dht/publications-full` and `:repairing? true`. The `/sent`\n  1272\t  fact for `a` then arrives, well before `:repair-ticks`: in that step the\n  1273\t  publication is `:republished :acknowledged`, with `:sent` equal to\n  1274\t  `:blobs`, no second request for `a` was issued, and no repair cycle of\n  1275\t  that publication ever opens. The same case with a second, unshared row\n  1276\t  yields `:republished :partial` on the fact, and `:acknowledged` after its\n  1277\t  own repair sends the row. A shared fact that is a failure changes no\n  1278\t  result and produces no event.\n  1279\t- **Terminal reasons stop repair.** A publication whose only failure is\n  1280\t  `/oversize` or `/absent` is reported once with `:repairing? false` and\n  1281\t  `:ended :dao.space.dht/terminal`, and no request for it is ever issued\n  1282\t  again. One with a terminal and a retryable failure repairs the retryable\n  1283\t  one and then ends `:terminal`, `:partial`.\n  1284\t- **`retry!`** makes the next cycle run at the next step without waiting\n  1285\t  for `:repair-ticks`, produces no event of its own, and is refused for a\n  1286\t  manifest that is not repairing.\n  1287\t- **`cancel!`, in each ledger state.** A publication cancelled with some\n  1288\t  blobs sent, some queued, some failed awaiting repair, and some\n  1289\t  outstanding is reported by the next step with `:ended\n  1290\t  :dao.space.dht/cancelled`, `:repairing? false`, the sent blobs under\n  1291\t  `:sent` and every other under `:failed` as `:dao.space.dht/cancelled`,\n  1292\t  and the invariant holds. The outstanding write's `/sent` fact then\n  1293\t  arrives: no further event for the cancelled manifest is produced and its\n  1294\t  reported counts are unchanged.\n  1295\t- **Shared addresses.** Two live publications holding one address issue\n  1296\t  one request; its outcome is written to both ledgers. Cancelling the\n  1297\t  first leaves the address queued for the second, which is later reported\n  1298\t  with that address `sent`; the late fact of an address outstanding at the\n  1299\t  cancel is counted by the second and not by the first.\n  1300\t- `cancel!` before the first report produces `:published`, after it\n  1301\t  `:republished`; it is refused for a manifest with no live publication.\n  1302\t- `close!` reports nothing, discards both queues, and leaves every blob\n  1303\t  local; a node reopened on the directory issues no repair request.\n  1304\t- A solo node and a non-publishing node hold no backlog and report\n  1305\t  `/solo` and `/unpublished` in the step that reads the puts.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"## Verdict: SIGN-OFF GRANTED\n\nR5 closes the remaining finding. A shared `/sent` fact now triggers `:republished` when it changes an overflow publication’s result, even before that publication’s repair cycle. The new L1 test covers acknowledgment, partial progress, and a shared failure without a duplicate request. The fixed-ledger accounting, queue bounds, and owner-approved automatic repair rules remain consistent.\n\n| Severity | File:line | Issue | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| None | — | No outstanding design findings. | — |"}}
